WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.136 --> 00:00:02.736
Hi, I'm Michele Schaefer.

2
00:00:02.736 --> 00:00:06.134
I'm the IT architect at Kestrel
Falls Community Hospital.

3
00:00:06.134 --> 00:00:10.922
I look after imaging, the ambulance
connections, and the third-party interfaces,

4
00:00:10.922 --> 00:00:13.864
all the places where Plexmoor
talks to another system.

5
00:00:13.864 --> 00:00:18.154
Sarah Wendt from Health Information Management
and I built the workbook you're

6
00:00:18.154 --> 00:00:19.598
about to open, so let me walk
you through it.

7
00:00:19.598 --> 00:00:21.173
Start with the Read me sheet.

8
00:00:21.173 --> 00:00:23.796
It explains every sheet and every column.

9
00:00:23.796 --> 00:00:25.686
Then there are four data sheets.

10
00:00:25.686 --> 00:00:30.894
Interfaces lists all thirty-eight connections
between Plexmoor and other systems.

11
00:00:30.894 --> 00:00:33.917
For each one you'll see which
way the data flows,

12
00:00:33.917 --> 00:00:36.265
about how many messages it sends a day,

13
00:00:36.265 --> 00:00:40.807
how many messages failed in the last ninety
days and needed a person to fix them,

14
00:00:40.807 --> 00:00:43.436
who owns it, and whether we'd rebuild it
or retire it with the new EHR.

15
00:00:43.436 --> 00:00:49.721
Data quality shows checks Sarah's team ran in
January on our patient records.

16
00:00:49.721 --> 00:00:52.498
Things like duplicates and
missing information,

17
00:00:52.498 --> 00:00:56.788
and what happens if we move those
records as they are.

18
00:00:56.788 --> 00:01:00.896
Monthly trends covers the last twelve months:
help desk tickets, interface errors,

19
00:01:00.896 --> 00:01:03.605
unplanned downtime, and
workaround reports.

20
00:01:03.605 --> 00:01:06.929
A workaround is when staff do
something outside the system,

21
00:01:06.929 --> 00:01:08.303
like writing vital signs on paper.

22
00:01:08.303 --> 00:01:13.453
And Workflows is what my team saw on
walk-throughs of units and clinics: how the

23
00:01:13.453 --> 00:01:16.510
work really gets done, and how
much it bothers people.

24
00:01:16.510 --> 00:01:18.191
Now, my worry.

25
00:01:18.191 --> 00:01:19.872
Interfaces break at go-live.

26
00:01:19.872 --> 00:01:23.618
And when an interface fails, nobody gets a
big red error on their screen.

27
00:01:23.618 --> 00:01:26.261
A lab result just doesn't show up.

28
00:01:26.261 --> 00:01:28.254
An image doesn't land in the chart.

29
00:01:28.254 --> 00:01:32.041
Somebody notices hours later, if
they notice at all.

30
00:01:32.041 --> 00:01:34.866
The other thing I've learned is
that bad data moves.

31
00:01:34.866 --> 00:01:40.667
If a record is a duplicate in Plexmoor, it'll be
a duplicate in the new system too,

32
00:01:40.667 --> 00:01:42.490
unless somebody cleans it up first.

33
00:01:42.490 --> 00:01:44.256
So here's what I'm asking for.

34
00:01:44.256 --> 00:01:45.767
An honest map.

35
00:01:45.767 --> 00:01:48.562
Don't make our current state look
prettier than it is,

36
00:01:48.562 --> 00:01:50.420
and don't make it look scarier either.

37
00:01:50.420 --> 00:01:51.803
Use the numbers.

38
00:01:51.803 --> 00:01:53.631
And when you put a number on a chart,

39
00:01:53.631 --> 00:01:56.868
tell Laura what it means for
patients and for the project.

40
00:01:56.868 --> 00:01:59.132
All of this data is fictional, by the way,

41
00:01:59.132 --> 00:02:04.249
but it's built to look like what a
hospital our size would really find.

42
00:02:04.249 --> 00:02:05.160
Treat it like it's real.

